r five years literally  the day that we were arrested July 22nd  2014 my wife and I she's also a  journalist had our accreditation  extended for another year on top of what  we had so you know I at various points  in those five years that I worked there  yes I was concerned about things that  were going on in the streets and around  us but I had gotten to that point where  I'd been living in this society for so  long knew the rules played by the rules  and this still happened it's amazing to  have you here and watch you talking so  that's just a blessing but  Viktor Frankl the famous writer about  the concentration camp said he who has a  why to live can bear almost any how yeah  so what was the why for you well first  and foremost you know being reunited  with my wife you know we had the good  fortune of her being in Iran as well and  after several months and and the raising  of awareness around my case I was given  the opportunity to see her from time to  time and she was you know real fuel of  that hope you know and she was the one  that was telling me hey you're never  gonna plead guilty you didn't do  anything wrong we're gonna get out of  this and you know we I think we  supported each other through a really  tough process and and knowing that that  the rest of the world was was really  behind us helped him thank goodness for  your brother thank your brother and your  mom he was really the true champion you  had many true champions but he kept your  name out there and I think people here  well you know you weren't physically  tortured so how bad could it have been I  really want people to understand exactly  how bad it was and what you went through  well literally from the first night Gale  they were telling me we're gonna cut  your head off I'm gonna behead you yeah  yeah and over over the the ensuing days  and you know when you're in solitary  confinement you're you blocked off from  from all reality they were telling me  that that had been already reported 